@chapter Crate @code{sotf-engine}
@cindex sotf-engine
@cindex audio engine

@code{sotf-engine} (library name @code{sotf_audio}) is the native
multi-threaded audio engine. It owns the decode / process / playback
pipeline, the plugin host wiring, the recorder and the device
abstraction.

@menu
* sotf-engine User Guide::
* sotf-engine Developer Guide::
@end menu

@node sotf-engine User Guide
@section User Guide

End users rarely touch the engine directly -- it is driven by the
desktop and TUI players. The interesting user-facing capabilities are:

@itemize @bullet
@item Multi-format playback: FLAC, MP3, AAC, WAV, AIFF, ALAC, Vorbis
  via Symphonia; optional IAMF via feature flag.
@item High-quality resampling via @code{rubato} for arbitrary device
  sample-rate conversion.
@item Optional live config reload from a watched file. Unix signal
  watching is opt-in and is intended for standalone processes.
@item Multi-channel recording for measurement.
@end itemize

Applications normally construct @code{AudioEngine} through the player
or server layers. DAWs and other hosts that own the audio callback use
@code{EmbeddedAudioEngine}, which processes caller-owned blocks against
an explicit external sample clock without opening a device.

@node sotf-engine Developer Guide
@section Developer Guide

@subheading Four-thread pipeline

@example
Manager  ──┬──> Decoder  ──> Processing  ──> Playback (cpal)
           │
           ├──> Config watcher / signal handler
           └──> Recorder
@end example

Bounded recycling queues carry audio buffers between stages. The cpal
hardware callback is created by the playback feeder thread but normally
runs on a backend-owned thread; neither path may allocate or lock.

@subheading Key types

@table @code
@item AudioEngine
The real-time pipeline owner. Construct with
@code{AudioEngine::new(EngineConfig)}, then use @code{play},
@code{pause}, @code{seek}, @code{set_volume}, and
@code{update_plugin_chain}. Dropping or explicitly shutting down the
engine stops its worker threads.
@item EmbeddedAudioEngine
Device-free plugin engine for a DAW render callback. Use
@code{process_at(sample_position, input, output)} for an external clock
and @code{set_plugin_parameter_at} for sample-offset automation in the
next block.
@item EngineConfig
Defines sample rate, frame and playback-buffer sizes, channels, output
device/access policy, plugins, network endpoint, oversampling, DSD
policy, and optional configuration watching.
@item AudioFrame
Owned @code{Vec<f32>} of interleaved samples for one quantum.
@item AudioEngineManager
Higher-level source and streaming lifecycle API used by player
frontends.
@item AudioDecoder
Decoder interface implemented by file, stream, PCM, DSD, and optional
IAMF sources.
@item CpalSink
Public standalone device sink. The four-thread engine also has an
engine-owned cpal builder because recovery and telemetry differ; both
paths share the same callback ring, gain-ramp, clamp, and dither
contracts.
@end table

@subheading Real-time discipline (mandatory)

@itemize @bullet
@item No allocations in @code{process()} for validated engine limits
  (at most 8192 frames and 16 declared I/O channels); pre-allocate in
  @code{build()}. Oversized configurations are rejected before startup.
@item No locks per frame; cache per-frame predicates (e.g.@:
  @code{has_variable_frame_plugin}) at build time.
@item STFT plugins return @code{context.num_frames}, not the actual
  draining count, so the ring buffer stays aligned.
@item Output clipping (@code{sample.clamp(-1.0, 1.0)}) belongs in the
  cpal callback, not inside plugins.
@item Volume and mute changes use a 10 ms callback-side ramp. Gain
  advances once per interleaved frame so every channel receives the
  same gain.
@item Pause flushes the playback ring immediately and discards upstream
  frames until resume. Only the hardware quantum already being rendered
  may finish; the configured software-buffer tail is not played.
@item Standalone @code{AudioEngine} parameter messages are
  block-granular. @code{EmbeddedAudioEngine} exposes the plugin host's
  sample-offset events for DAW automation. Fixed-rate, fixed-frame
  chains split eligible blocks at event offsets; variable-rate/frame
  chains apply queued offsets at the next safe block boundary.
@item On macOS, CoreAudio schedules its render thread. On Linux and
  Windows, applications must verify the selected backend's callback
  scheduling. The playback feeder deliberately uses normal scheduling;
  engine priority elevation applies only to processing workers, not to
  a cpal-owned callback thread.
@item Debug builds on macOS must keep @code{opt-level = 0}; aggressive
  optimisation breaks cpal/CoreAudio device enumeration.
@end itemize

@subheading Layout

@example
crates/sotf-engine/
  src/
    engine/                pipeline + threads
    decoder/               file/stream decoders, DSD, sample-rate hop
    plugins/               engine plugin configuration and conversion
    manager/               high-level playback/streaming manager
    signal_recorder/       multi-channel measurement capture
    timeline/              tracks, transport, automation and MIDI model
    offline_renderer/      offline WAV rendering
    project/               project model and timeline bridge
@end example

@subheading Build flags

@itemize @bullet
@item Defaults: @code{streaming}, @code{hls}, and @code{iamf}.
@item @code{streaming} enables outbound HTTP PCM streaming;
  @code{hls} adds HLS support.
@item @code{iamf} feature enables IAMF support.
@item @code{hal} enables the macOS systemwide HAL integration.
@item @code{asio} enables the Windows ASIO backend.
@item @code{playback-runtime-harness} exposes only the internal
  allocation/fuzz harness and must not be enabled in production.
@end itemize

@subheading Embedding and global process state

@code{EngineConfig::watch_config} defaults to @code{false} and controls
only file watching. @code{EngineConfig::watch_signals} is a separate
opt-in for process-global SIGINT, SIGTERM, and SIGHUP handlers; keep it
disabled inside a DAW, plugin, or other host process. Standalone
@code{AudioEngine} uses cpal device ownership.
@code{EmbeddedAudioEngine} is the device-free host-driven API; the DAW
owns its callback, clock, and output sink. It declares a maximum block
size from @code{EngineConfig::frame_size}; call @code{reset_transport}
on discontinuities and loop jumps.

@subheading Additional public subsystems and status

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{timeline}, @code{offline_renderer}, and @code{project}
  provide pull-based project rendering. They are not wired into the
  four-thread real-time pipeline.
@item External CLAP, VST3, and Audio Unit hosting uses isolated worker
  processes with platform sandbox status reported by the plugin layer.
@item Plugin delay compensation and engine oversampling policy are
  applied while building the host chain.
@item DSF/DFF decode to PCM is supported. Native DSD and DoP output are
  reported as unavailable/fallback according to @code{DsdOutputMode}.
@item The network endpoint implements outbound HTTP PCM streaming.
  Input-client mode is planned, not implemented.
@item iOS playback uses a real RemoteIO AudioUnit callback despite the
  historical @code{playback_thread_stub} module name. Device discovery,
  config watching, and runtime output reconfiguration remain limited.
@end itemize

@subheading Testing

@example
cargo test -p sotf-engine
cargo check -p sotf-engine
cargo clippy -p sotf-engine -- -W warnings

@c Internal callback/frame allocation harness:
cargo test -p sotf-engine --features playback-runtime-harness \
  --test playback_runtime_allocation_tests
@end example
